{
  "$comment": "Identity of this blueprint. In the source repository role is 'source'. In an adopting project, sync-blueprint rewrites this file with role 'adopted', the source it came from, and a hash per synced file so local customizations can be detected instead of silently overwritten.",
  "role": "source",
  "version": "1.20.0",
  "releasedAt": "2026-09-28",
  "changelog": {
    "1.20.0": "Closure integrity, a brief that pays its own budget, and one mandatory-rule contract across both shells. (1) Lifecycle. finish-task resolves its target and its related plan to a real path before acting and refuses anything that resolves outside .ai/tasks/ or .ai/plans/, so a symlink or a junction cannot walk a closure out of the repository. Writes are staged: both destinations are written before any original is removed, and a failure leaves the records byte for byte rather than half closed. Two different records sharing one name are reported as a CONFLICT with exit 2 and neither is touched -- that is conflict PRESERVATION, and it is not the same thing as recovery. Recovery is the separate case where the two records differ only by what a closure itself would have changed: that one is completed rather than refused, and repeating it is safe. Both behaviours are pinned by fixture cases in the self-test, and the recovery case is where the recovery evidence comes from. A plan named by more than one active task stays active until the last of them closes; when it is finally archived a forwarding record is left at the old path carrying Status moved and Now at, so references written before the move keep resolving. A forwarding record is not a plan and is not counted as one. Evidence is required at closure, and a waiver is a structured field -- scope, reason, risk, and a reference that must resolve to a file in the repository -- which waives exactly one named field and nothing else. (2) The brief pays its own budget. forgeos brief measures the text it is about to print and keeps itself inside an 800-token target by dropping OPTIONAL content whole, in a fixed order, and naming what it dropped. Mandatory content is never touched: the guardrails, the identity of the active capability, and the blocker line when one stands. When the mandatory content alone will not fit, the brief prints it whole and reports MANDATORY OVERFLOW rather than truncating a rule or claiming a fit it did not achieve. Every figure is UTF-8 bytes divided by four and is labelled an estimate wherever it appears, in the output, in the tests and in this entry. It is an approximation of size, not a measured model token count, and no session-wide saving is claimed anywhere. The measurement includes the notice the brief adds about its own shortening, so the number printed describes the text that was printed. (3) One mandatory-rule contract, both shells. The Do not list handed to a session differed between PowerShell and POSIX, and had since the section existed. Three causes, each reproduced on a shared fixture before anything was changed: a bullet indented with a tab was not a bullet to the POSIX parser, so a prohibition an author had written was dropped in silence while the other shell emitted it; when-not matched its subject case-insensitively on one shell and case-sensitively on the other; and a tab, a doubled space, or a colon with nothing after it inside a when-not entry sent a well-formed rule down the malformed path on POSIX, where it printed verbatim and its condition never ran. A bullet is now a dash followed by a run of spaces or tabs, the text is trimmed the same way on both shells, when-not matches without regard to case, and its separators are equally tolerant. The brief prints the rules the way its own JSON doNot array reports them, which also brought the two shells token estimates into agreement. Five label-identical cases per shell assert the rules themselves -- exact membership and order from a known fixture, in the brief, in the session package and in the JSON. Self-test 241 to 246 cases per shell, same labels, same order. COMPATIBILITY, read this before upgrading. (a) Tooling older than this version counts every file in .ai/plans/active/ as an active plan, so a forwarding record left by this version will be counted as a plan by an older reader; the count is wrong there, not here. (b) A waiver written in the older free-text form is REJECTED: closure now requires scope, reason, risk and a resolving reference. An in-flight task carrying a legacy waiver will refuse to close until the field is rewritten. (c) Conditional-rule parsing and whitespace handling changed: a when-not pattern that relied on case-sensitive matching now suppresses its rule where it did not before, and rule text is trimmed, so a project that pads its bullets will see slightly different prompt text. No rule is removed and none is added by the change itself. (d) Brief rendering changed -- the bullet marker is gone from the rule block -- and the brief JSON gained fields (reductionLevel, shortened, mandatoryOverflow, overflowTokens). The additions are additive and existing fields keep their meaning, but a consumer that pinned the exact brief text will see a diff. UPGRADING. Run the supported update as a dry run first, read the list, then apply: forgeos update --target <project> and then the same command with --apply. Files a project customized are skipped rather than overwritten unless --force is given, and project-owned content -- context, tasks, plans, memory, docs, README -- is never touched. Before upgrading, search active task records for a waiver in the old form and rewrite it. After upgrading, expect the brief to be shorter and to name what it shortened. KNOWN LIMITATIONS. The acceptance review that cleared this batch was performed by the same agent that wrote it; it was not an independent review and no second party examined the code. One adopted project of three was used for the trial, pinned at a committed snapshot, and its closure scenarios ran on explicitly synthetic records because that snapshot held no active task -- no operational closure has happened in any real project, and that field trial remains pending. Structured waivers were exercised on fixtures only. Separately, and not introduced by this release: a project whose documentation references its own generated build output will fail the link check in any clean checkout, including CI, because the generated files are not in version control; this was observed during the trial and is a pre-existing condition of such projects, left for them to resolve. The documented compatibility matrix -- which adopted versions may upgrade to which -- is still outstanding.",
    "1.19.0": "Project intake, and a CI guard adopters were failing on. (1) forgeos intake: a read-only report, also intake --json, that says what kind of project this is before an agent reads anything. Five modes -- PROJECT_WITH_GOVERNING_DOCS, PROJECT_DISCOVERY_REQUIRED, CODEBASE_RECONSTRUCTION_REQUIRED, WEBSITE_PROJECT, ENTERPRISE_SYSTEM, and NOT_APPLICABLE in the source blueprint -- each with a confidence, tags for the other modes that also apply, and capped evidence paths. (2) Readiness forecast: one line each for product truth, architecture, data, governance, and implementation, every one a status (ready, partial, missing, blocked, n/a) with a short reason, beside map coverage from .ai/product and the code, website, and enterprise signals found. (3) Token risk: low, medium, or high, weighed from always-loaded bytes / 4, governing documents over 200 KB, and governing documents with no maps. It reports a risk and never claims a saving; no multiplier is stated anywhere. (4) The next prompt family, with the one file to read first: extract project intelligence, run discovery, reconstruct from codebase, website review/build/deploy, enterprise module/phasing, blocked owner decision, or safe implementation. The whole report is built from names, sizes, and presence: it opens blueprint.version, .ai/context/project.md, and .ai/context/governance.json, never a governing document, and its directory walk stops at depth 3 and 5000 directories with dependency and build directories pruned. It writes nothing and authorizes nothing -- the JSON carries forgeos.project-intake/1 with canModifyFiles, canAuthorizeCode, and canOpenGovernanceWindow all false -- and the two shells produce identical output, pinned by five self-test cases each: 216 to 221 cases per shell, label-identical. (5) Install matrix adopter guard, the defect this release exists to fix for adopted projects. validate.yml is portable, but its two install-matrix jobs build a release artifact with tooling that is source-only and never reaches an adopting project, so every adopter inherited two jobs that could not pass, and a red Validate Blueprint run stopped meaning anything. Each job now records whether its own builder is present immediately after checkout -- hashFiles cannot guard the job itself, whose condition is evaluated before checkout -- and every later step runs only when it is; elsewhere the job ends green with a notice naming why. In this repository the matrix still runs in full, and a release self-test case fails if any step escapes the guard: 14 to 15 cases per shell. Confirmed in the field on the ForgeOS website project, which had been failing those jobs and reported Validate Blueprint 8 of 8 after updating, the install-matrix jobs green instead of red. Upgrading is the fix: an adopted project on 1.18.0 or earlier keeps failing those two jobs until it syncs this version.",
    "1.18.0": "The Project Ingestion Layer, slice 1: a project's own documents become compact, cited maps an agent reads first. (1) Two modes, decided by what the repository holds. PROJECT_WITH_GOVERNING_DOCS -- a client specification, developer documents, a data model -- builds seven maps in .ai/product/: authority map, source index, project concept, module map, requirement matrix, implementation roadmap, and open decisions. PROJECT_DISCOVERY_REQUIRED -- no governing documents -- prepares six discovery records there instead: project brief, stakeholders, module map draft, questions, assumptions, and phase roadmap. (2) The ingest-project workflow (.ai/workflows/ingest-project.md) defines the procedure: classify by listing files without opening them, rank sources with a default authority order in which signed specifications win and code is authoritative only for what is, index every source once with a stable ID and cite it as S-03 section 4.2, extract in compact words while leaving whole passages in the source, one-line requirements with an acceptance hint, and open decisions raised only after the sources were searched. (3) Thirteen templates in templates/project-intelligence/, seven per the first mode and six per the second. (4) check-project-ingestion (.ps1 and .sh): an informational validation row that reports the mode, the governing directories found, and map coverage (k of 7 or k of 6). It checks presence only, never opens a document, always exits 0, and reports N/A in the source blueprint. The authority map counts as a declaration when governing documents live elsewhere. check-all now reports twelve rows: eight gating, four informational. check-links ignores project-owned .ai/product/ references and check-structure does not report those files as undeclared. (5) Token economy guardrails: economy.md rule 8 reads documented projects through their maps -- maps first, the original only to verify the one passage a citation names, never a specification copied into always-loaded context, never a whole document re-read for what a map already cites, and never a question to the owner before the sources were searched. (6) Self-test grows from 214 to 216 cases per shell, label-identical: one pair per mode, built on throwaway fixtures that carry their own blueprint.version so the answer never depends on the host. Deferred and tracked as M-25 in docs/roadmap.md: forgeos ingest and a slash command (slice 2), and gating once proven on adopters (slice 3).",
    "1.17.0": "Sessions bounded end to end, and the launcher that starts them. (1) Large-session protocol: .ai/contract/economy.md section 4 adds an eight-rule whole-session bound -- single agent, read by section with the reason stated, effort per decision, validate once per change, cross-platform runs and selftest only on trigger, rehearse SQL once, compact output, and stop in the usage-risk zone -- written after one large implementation session on an adopted project reached its usage limit despite starting from a brief within budget. The implement workflow carries its shape. It relaxes no validation, review, or gate, and it is itself unmeasured (open question 006). (2) Session policy tuning: scripts/lib/session-policy.json is provider-neutral -- recommended models are named 'frontier' and efforts use lower-case tiers -- and implementation categories default to medium effort, raised per decision rather than per session. (3) forgeos brief: a compact session launcher, also prompt --brief, cut to at most 800 tokens and measured by the command itself (566 tokens against 2,429 for the full prompt here, UTF-8 bytes / 4). Both brief and prompt carry the one-line form of the large-session protocol. When the next capability is blocked, the brief names the blocker and either a task record already waiting in .ai/tasks/inbox/ or that no safe unblocked alternative was found; it invents nothing, and prompt output is otherwise unchanged. (4) Governance clarification: an independent review is still required, but the review workflow no longer implies spawning one -- Rule #9 wins. An authorized reviewer receives a role packet (build-context --role, about a quarter of the full context as measured) instead of the repository, and a self-review records the gap explicitly as a finding. reporting.md is tiered: a three-section report for minor tasks, the full eight sections otherwise. (5) Validation and tooling: guard-discovery fails closed on a missing or unreadable manifest; check-placeholders.sh probes its manifest reader by executing it, so a Windows Store python stub can no longer report zero blocking markers; check-context-budget reports the platform descriptors separately from the project's files; CI captures the self-test with --verbose / -Verbose so the parity job compares real case lists under compact-by-default output. The self-test grows from 210 to 214 cases per shell, label-identical. (6) Adopter readiness: three cold-session brief trials on one adopted project are recorded as anonymized field evidence -- M-23 row 9 done, row 8 partial -- and the next safe step is a second adopted project resuming from files alone.",
    "1.16.0": "The token economy overhaul, and the governance that goes with it. A minor release rather than a patch, because it changes the operating contract, the adopter templates, and the default behaviour of the validation commands. (1) Compact validation output: check-all and selftest now default to a compact mode that prints the summary table and the verdict, and full detail only on failure -- measured at up to 38.7x fewer characters (27,563 to 712) and 23.5x fewer lines on a passing run. Pass --verbose / -Verbose to restore the old output; --compact / -Compact is accepted explicitly. Nothing about what is checked changed: the same eleven rows, the same gating split, the same case count. (2) Anti-swarm governance: Non-Negotiable Rule #9 forbids autonomous subagents, background review swarms, and recursive task loops without explicit authorization, and defaults to single-agent execution with bounded context. The Efficiency Standard gained three token-frugality mandates -- no repository-wide file dumps, compact validation by default, and stop expanding context once the evidence is sufficient. The rule is injected into every generated session package, so a prompt produced by forgeos prompt carries it as a named constraint rather than as advice. (3) Discovery no longer delegates: the architecture phase completes in the current session or asks a human architect, in both the contract and the workflow, because a step that required a subagent contradicted the rule that forbids one. (4) Adopter templates gained the same defaults -- the constraints template now carries 'spawn autonomous subagents or parallel review swarms' and 'unconstrained repo-wide file dumps' as Always prohibitions, keeps its 'add your own' guidance, and states the two prohibition forms in a way that renders correctly. (5) A self-test defect found by a real web adopter: the POSIX-installer case asserted that no package.json exists at the repository root, which is true of this repository and false of any Node or Astro project that adopts ForgeOS. The assertion now sits behind the role check, as every source-specific expectation must. (6) Always-loaded context was trimmed into its recorded budget, and a 300-line boundedness rule for logs and ledgers was written into the documentation rules; check-context-budget is documented as informational by default, gating only under --fail-on-over / -FailOnOver, which is what it has always done.",
    "1.15.31": "forgeos prompt: the session package. The command centre could already say WHAT to do next; the person starting the session still had to decide the rest -- same session or new, what name, which model, what effort, what to read first, what is in and out of scope, what the report must contain. The engine now answers all of it: project-status gained --section prompt, and forgeos prompt routes to it exactly as status and next do, byte-identical by assertion. The package is host-aware and refuses to invent: the capability comes from the roadmap criteria table, the session decision from the work in flight, the scope from the governance file, the prohibitions from the project constraints, and model plus effort from scripts/lib/session-policy.json -- a new portable data table, one category per line, first match wins, the last entry the default, adopter-editable by design; the names in it are session recommendations, never authorship claims. Push, tag and deploy can only ever say refused or ask-the-owner; commit is governance-aware and says allowed local-only solely while the gate is open. When the roadmap names no incomplete row, or the ledger, governance file or policy table is missing, both surfaces exit 1 naming each missing source and its remedy instead of printing a prompt built on guesses. The one-category-per-line contract is enforced on both shells -- a reformatted table means a named refusal, identically, everywhere -- the push probe matches case-insensitively, the tag probe is word-anchored, and the PowerShell half matches culture-invariantly so a machine locale cannot change the category. Eight new self-test cases per shell pin all of it: 202 to 210, same labels, same order.",
    "1.15.30": "The launch-day review, closed the day it ran. An independent review of the published snapshot found two documents carrying more of the pre-launch line than intended: the discovery example, now rewritten around a declared fiction (a hobbyist ceramics course platform, with examples/README.md stating outright that every scenario in the directory is invented), and the readiness review, now a dated summary of what the pre-launch audit verified rather than the working audit itself. With them, the accuracy fixes the same review measured: the second policy-control figure on the front page now matches the tool (144), the token-economy figure matches the meter (3.5k), the front page no longer says the Command Center does not exist below the section documenting its shipped engine, the roadmap no longer opens the installability section by denying the five Proven rows under it, and the self-test suite headers no longer claim a case count three digits stale. No check weakened; 202 cases in both shells, same labels, same order.",
    "1.15.29": "Initial public release of ForgeOS. This repository is a clean, verified snapshot of the source blueprint: 11 validation rows (8 gating), 202 self-test cases per shell proven label-identical by CI, a release workflow that builds a checksummed artifact from a tag, installers for Windows and Linux proven end to end by an install matrix, and a local forgeos command wrapping the sync engine. Development history before this release lives in the private development line; from here, every change lands in this changelog."
  },
  "requires": {
    "powershell": ">=5.1",
    "bash": ">=4.0",
    "git": ">=2.0",
    "jqOrPython3": "required by the .sh variants only"
  }
}
